{
  "file": "crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Contents requires this callback even for a storage-only consumer. The\n delegation test must use only node_path; discovering edges would be\n an unwanted graph access, which this guard rejects. Traversal fixtures\n separately exercise edge discovery."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]` is a valid coverage exception.\n\n- **Form:** The target is an explicit function with a body. The justification is in fenced citation commentary, so it is a tracked explanation and not a bare comment. The function carries no other `{impl}` or `{test}` reference, so the citation is not redundant.\n- **Substance:** The function is a deliberate guard that panics. It exists so the storage-delegation test fails if storage code touches graph edges. The reason is not just that the code is hard to cover.\n- **Agreement with the source:** The commentary holds up against the code.\n  - `Graph` in `crates/habeas-sdk/src/graph.rs` requires `outgoing_edges`. `Contents: Graph` inherits that requirement, so the wording \"Contents requires this callback\" is loose but accurate.\n  - In `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` and `malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors`, `StoredGraph` is used only through `node_path`, `load_evidence`, and `store_evidence`. `StoredReview::execute` never discovers edges. So the guard is never run, and the panic would only fire on an unwanted graph access.\n  - Other fixtures do implement `outgoing_edges` and exercise edge discovery: `impl[Memory/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]` in `tests/validation.rs`, and the equivalent items in `tests/agent.rs` and `tests/contracts.rs`.",
